= Wave 13 context capture =

'''State: Phase 1 — Context capture.'''

Wave 13 establishes the public Internet/Web/platform substrate that sits above the existing [[Computer network]], [[Distributed system]], [[Computer security]], [[Identity]], and [[Governance]] corpus.

== Normalized scope ==

# [[Internet]]
# [[World Wide Web]]
# [[Protocol (computing)]]
# [[Internet protocol suite]]
# [[Domain Name System]]
# [[Uniform Resource Identifier]]
# [[Hypertext Transfer Protocol]]
# [[Client-server model]]
# [[Web browser]]
# [[Web server]]
# [[Application programming interface]]
# [[Authentication]]
# [[Authorization]]
# [[Interoperability]]
# [[Federation (information technology)]]
# [[Online platform]]
# [[Content moderation]]

== Title decisions ==

* [[Protocol (computing)]] is qualified because protocol has broad non-computing meanings.
* [[Federation (information technology)]] is qualified because federation also has political and organizational meanings.
* [[Uniform Resource Identifier]] is canonical rather than “URL”; URLs are an important URI use/subset but not the whole concept.
* [[Application programming interface]] is canonical rather than the abbreviation API.
* [[Online platform]] is used for the durable sociotechnical service layer rather than naming a specific commercial platform.

== Domain decision ==

No new controlled Domain is introduced. Wave 13 uses existing Computer science, Technology, Systems science, and where appropriate Engineering.

== Dependency graph ==

<pre>
Internet
 ├→ Internet protocol suite
 │    ├→ Protocol (computing)
 │    └→ Domain Name System
 └→ World Wide Web
      ├→ Uniform Resource Identifier
      ├→ Hypertext Transfer Protocol
      ├→ Client-server model
      │    ├→ Web browser
      │    └→ Web server
      └→ Application programming interface

Identity / access
 ├→ Authentication
 └→ Authorization

Cross-system operation
 ├→ Interoperability
 └→ Federation (information technology)

Platform layer
 └→ Online platform
      └→ Content moderation
</pre>

This graph is explanatory, not a strict subclass hierarchy.

== Required non-equivalences ==

<pre>
Internet ≠ World Wide Web
protocol ≠ implementation
Internet protocol suite ≠ Internet itself
DNS ≠ the Web
domain name ≠ URI
URI ≠ network location necessarily
HTTP ≠ HTML
HTTP ≠ the Web
client/server role ≠ permanent machine identity
web browser ≠ search engine
web server ≠ physical server machine necessarily
API ≠ network protocol necessarily
authentication ≠ authorization
authorization ≠ authentication
interoperability ≠ identical implementation
federation ≠ decentralization
federation ≠ single sign-on
online platform ≠ Internet
content moderation ≠ proof that moderated content is false or harmful
</pre>

== Source families ==

* IETF RFCs — Internet architecture, DNS, URI syntax, HTTP semantics, and protocol terminology.
* W3C Web Architecture — Web identification, interaction, representation, and agent/resource model.
* NIST SP 800-63-4 family — current authentication and federation terminology.
* NIST access-control literature — authorization/access-control distinctions.
* W3C ActivityPub Recommendation — concrete federated client/server and server/server architecture.
* Platform-governance literature — online platforms and content moderation as sociotechnical systems.

== Cross-wave links ==

* [[Computer network]], [[Distributed system]], [[Computer security]], [[Cryptography]], and [[Public-key cryptography]] provide infrastructure/security foundations.
* [[Identity]], [[Decentralized identity]], and [[Attestation]] remain distinct from authentication and authorization.
* [[Governance]], [[Reputation system]], [[Sybil attack]], and [[Multi-agent system]] connect to platform operation without defining the platform layer.
* [[Semantic Web]], [[Resource Description Framework]], and [[Web Ontology Language]] remain Web applications/standards built on the broader Web substrate.

== Future normalized redlinks ==

Internet Protocol, Transmission Control Protocol, User Datagram Protocol, IPv4, IPv6, routing, autonomous system, Border Gateway Protocol, URL, HTML, TLS, cookie, session, OAuth, OpenID Connect, access control, identity provider, single sign-on, social network, recommendation system, trust and safety, terms of service, platform governance.

These are coverage signals, not authorization for stubs.

== Phase-1 decisions ==

# Keep Wave 13 at 17 canonical concepts.
# Use the durable Internet/Web/platform layer rather than vendor-specific products.
# Preserve Internet/Web, URI/URL, HTTP/Web, browser/search-engine, API/protocol, authentication/authorization, federation/decentralization, and moderation/truth boundaries.
# Do not create a new Domain.
# Treat standards by actual publication status rather than as generic “current standards.”

